**Black River** is both a village and the name of the district that covers the whole west coast described on this site. The village lies on the coast between Tamarin and La Gaulette where the Black River reaches the sea below a ridge of mountains.

## La Preneuse and the Martello tower

La Preneuse public beach is named after the French frigate *Preneuse* which sailed from the island in the late 18th century when Mauritius was the French Isle de France. On the beach stands a Martello tower built by the British in 1832 and 1833. After taking the island from France in 1810 Britain built five of these round stone towers along the west coast to guard against a French return. Only three survive. This one was restored and opened to the public in the 1990s and is the best preserved. It now tells the story of the island's military and colonial past.

## Big game fishing

Black River is the island's main centre for big game fishing. Deep water lies close to the coast here and boats head out beyond the reef for blue and black marlin and tuna and other large fish. The main season runs roughly from November to April.

## Black River Gorges National Park

Inland rises the largest national park in Mauritius. It was proclaimed in 1994 and covers about 67 km² of forest and ravines and heathland with some 60 km of trails. It shelters all of the island's endemic birds still living such as the Mauritius kestrel and the pink pigeon and the echo parakeet. The highest summit in Mauritius rises above it: the Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire (828 m). Trails start both near the coast and from the plateau around Plaine Champagne.

## Getting there

Black River is roughly an hour by car from the international airport and a few minutes south of Tamarin along the coast road.

## Good to know

- Start forest hikes early: it is cooler and the light is better for spotting birds.
- Take water and rain gear: the uplands can be wet even when the coast is dry.
